    "updated_at": "2021-04-12T19:53:23Z",
    "body": "Hmm... Although I'm very tempted to \"dig in\" and give a sentence-by-sentence analysis, I'm afraid I don't have time to do this.  Instead, I will just give a few bullet points:\r\n\r\n-  Although it does a wonderful job providing a detailed description of the technical specifications, the biggest issue I noticed is that it doesn't seem to be written in a way that considers the programmer's point of view.  For example, regarding the \"autofill detail tokens\", the text provides exampes of its use, which is great, but in my opinion, it ought to also provide examples of the results of said code.  This is what I mean by writing in a way that consider's the programmer's point of view: a programmer like myself is much more interested in the results of a block of code, not so much its technical details.\r\n- Jargon is fine; it's a necessary evil.  However, the writing would be clearer if a) jargon was used only when there is no other alternative, and b) if jargon must be used, link to it's definition or define it in text (or even better, a hover-over popup that contains the definition).\r\n\r\nHope this helps!  Best of luck.",
